+ {"class_id": 3103, "class": "wasm-phi-liveness-lowering-structured-control-flow", "title": "wasm-phi-liveness-lowering-structured-control-flow", "description": "wasm-phi-liveness-lowering-structured-control-flow", "answer_id": 4838, "language": "python", "environment": "python3", "version": "3", "solution": "# Fixing SSA Phi Lowering for WebAssembly Structured Control Flow\n\n**Problem class:** `wasm-phi-liveness-lowering-structured-control-flow`\n**Environment:** Python 3.14, WABT 1.0.36 (`wat2wasm`, `wasm-validate`), Node 22\n**Artifact:** a self-contained lowering + a differential verifier (files also in `~/wasm_phi/`).\n\n---\n\n## TL;DR\n\nA front end that lowers SSA to WebAssembly fails in four distinct ways when handling phi nodes, critical edges, irreducible loops and multi-value results:\n\n1. **Phi copies are edge properties, but they are placed at blocks.** A copy appended at the end of a predecessor runs on *every* outgoing edge, so on a **critical edge** it clobbers a sibling successor's value.\n2. **Phi copies are simultaneous, but they are emitted sequentially.** A dependence cycle (`p := q, q := p`, or any loop-carried swap) needs a temporary or the update is lost.\n3. **Liveness under-approximates.** Edge-move operands, terminator uses (return values / branch conditions) and *parameters live on entry* are not counted. Because liveness drives spill-slot colouring and copy coalescing, two simultaneously-live values can share a local and the second write destroys the first. This is the \"loop accumulator silently reverts\" / \"returned tuple is `[x, x]`\" failure.\n4. **Irreducible loops are not lexically nestable.** WebAssembly only has `block`/`loop`/`if` and branches to *enclosing* labels; a multi-entry cycle cannot be expressed by naive nesting without duplication. A dispatch-state (`loop` + `br_table`) lowering is the general, always-valid structured form.\n\nThe fix is a five-stage pipeline: **critical-edge splitting → out-of-SSA parallel copies → correct liveness/interference colouring → structured dispatch lowering → multi-value-aware WAT emission.** It executes blocks in exactly the source order, so observable traps and evaluation order are preserved.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Symptom\n\n| Symptom | Failing shape | Wrong | Correct |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lost loop accumulator | `loop_sum` after 1 iteration | `0` | `1` |\n| Loop-carried value reverted | `irreducible` | `0` | expected |\n| Returned tuple is `[x, x]` | two values defined in a `Return` block | `[-2, -2]` | `[-1, -2]` |\n| Phi merge corrupted | diamond whose arms share a local | `[0, 0]` | `[2, 0]` |\n| Validation failure | irreducible / multi-entry loop | invalid wasm | valid module |\n| Spurious trap | division after a lost spill | no trap | `integer divide by zero` |\n\n## 2. Root-cause analysis\n\n### 2.1 A phi is an *edge* function, not a *block* function\nAn edge `P → V` is **critical** when `outdegree(P) > 1 and indegree(V) > 1`. Copying a phi input at the end of `P` also runs when `P` branches elsewhere; sinking it into `V` cannot tell `P` from other predecessors. **Fix:** split every critical edge with a fresh empty block `S`: `P → S → V`. Rewrite `V.phi.incoming[P]` to `S` and append all copies to `S`.\n\n### 2.2 Phi copies are simultaneous\nAll phis of `V` on edge `P → V` execute in parallel. Emitting them one-by-one is wrong whenever a destination is another move's source (swap / rotation). **Fix:** the classic parallel-move schedule — emit any move whose destination no pending move still reads; if none exists, break the cycle by stashing the destination's old value in a fresh temp and rewriting readers. This is `sequentialize()`.\n\n### 2.3 Liveness drives coalescing, so under-approximating it is a miscompile\nThe allocator builds an interference graph: whenever a variable is defined while another is live, they interfere and may not share a slot. Missing edges allow two live values to share a local. Three easy-to-miss classes:\n\n* **(a) Edge-move operands** — a value used only by an edge's copies is live at the end of the predecessor but is **not** in `live_in(successor)`. The scan must be seeded with `move_sources ∪ (live_in(succ) \\ move_defs)`.\n* **(b) Terminator uses** — a `Return` block has empty live-out; two values defined and both returned never interfere unless the returned values are seeded. Branch conditions likewise.\n* **(c) Parameters** — no defining instruction, so the \"edge at definition\" rule never fires. Make every pair in `live_in(entry)` interfere.\n\n### 2.4 Irreducible loops cannot be lexically nested\nA multi-entry cycle has no unique header, so no single lexical `loop` dominates its blocks. **Fix:** lower to a structured dispatch state machine — number blocks, keep a `pc` local, wrap each block in a nested `block` whose `end` is its entry, and jump with `br_table`; each body sets the next `pc` and `br`s to the dispatch `loop`. Every `br` targets an enclosing label, so it validates for *any* CFG. Block execution order is preserved exactly.\n\n### 2.5 Multi-value\nEvery phi is an independent scalar channel; copies are scalar; a `Return(v0…vk)` becomes `(result i32)*k` with `(return …)`. Values travel through locals/returns, so dispatcher blocks have arity `[] → []`.\n\n### 2.6 Why traps/order are preserved\nInstructions stay in order; edge moves run only on their edge, after the condition is read (`if` pops the condition before an arm runs); dispatch happens at block end, so no trapping instruction is reordered; `i32.div_s`/`i32.rem_s` trap at the identical point. Unreachable blocks are still emitted well-typed.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The fix: pipeline\n\n```\nSSA IR\n  |  split_critical_edges()   # unique edge per phi input\n  |  collect_edge_moves()     # parallel copies, cycle-safe\n  |  build_lowered()          # non-SSA instructions\n  |  compute_liveness()       # use/def incl. edge moves + terminators\n  |  build_interference()     # + entry clique for parameters\n  |  color_variables()        # min locals + coalescing\n  |  emit WAT                 # dispatch + multi-value returns\n```\n\n## 4. Exact code\n\n### `solution.py`\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nwasm-phi-liveness-lowering-structured-control-flow\n==================================================\n\nA self-contained reference implementation that lowers an SSA IR with\nphi nodes, critical edges, irreducible loops and multi-value blocks into\n*valid* WebAssembly structured control flow while preserving the exact\nevaluation order and trap behaviour of the source program.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport copy\nimport json\nimport os\nimport shutil\nimport subprocess\nimport tempfile\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\nfrom typing import Any, Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\n\n# =============================================================================\n# 1.  SSA IR\n# =============================================================================\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Phi:\n    \"\"\"`dest = phi(pred_0: v_0, pred_1: v_1, ...)`.\"\"\"\n    dest: str\n    incoming: Dict[str, Any]  # predecessor block name -> value (str name or int)\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Inst:\n    \"\"\"`dest = op(args...)` (pure, total except for the trapping div/rem ops).\"\"\"\n    dest: str\n    op: str\n    args: List[Any]\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Jump:\n    target: str\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass CondBr:\n    cond: str\n    then_bb: str\n    else_bb: str\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Return:\n    values: List[Any]\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Block:\n    name: str\n    phis: List[Phi] = field(default_factory=list)\n    insts: List[Inst] = field(default_factory=list)\n    term: Any = None\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass Function:\n    name: str\n    params: List[str]\n    blocks: List[Block]\n    entry: str\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# 2.  Reference semantics (used both as oracle and to define traps)\n# =============================================================================\n\n\nclass WasmTrap(Exception):\n    \"\"\"A WebAssembly trap (divide-by-zero, integer overflow).\"\"\"\n\n\ndef wrap32(x: int) -> int:\n    x &= 0xFFFFFFFF\n    return x - 0x100000000 if x >= 0x80000000 else x\n\n\ndef _trunc_div(x: int, y: int) -> int:\n    q = abs(x) // abs(y)\n    return q if (x < 0) == (y < 0) else -q\n\n\ndef eval_op(op: str, a: List[int]) -> int:\n    if op == \"const\":\n        return wrap32(a[0])\n    if op == \"add\":\n        return wrap32(a[0] + a[1])\n    if op == \"sub\":\n        return wrap32(a[0] - a[1])\n    if op == \"mul\":\n        return wrap32(a[0] * a[1])\n    if op == \"div_s\":\n        if a[1] == 0:\n            raise WasmTrap(\"integer divide by zero\")\n        if a[0] == -(2 ** 31) and a[1] == -1:\n            raise WasmTrap(\"integer overflow\")\n        return wrap32(_trunc_div(a[0], a[1]))\n    if op == \"div_u\":\n        if a[1] == 0:\n            raise WasmTrap(\"integer divide by zero\")\n        return wrap32((a[0] & 0xFFFFFFFF) // (a[1] & 0xFFFFFFFF))\n    if op == \"rem_s\":\n        if a[1] == 0:\n            raise WasmTrap(\"integer divide by zero\")\n        if a[0] == -(2 ** 31) and a[1] == -1:\n            return 0\n        return wrap32(a[0] - _trunc_div(a[0], a[1]) * a[1])\n    if op == \"rem_u\":\n        if a[1] == 0:\n            raise WasmTrap(\"integer divide by zero\")\n        return wrap32((a[0] & 0xFFFFFFFF) % (a[1] & 0xFFFFFFFF))\n    if op in (\"and\", \"or\", \"xor\"):\n        x, y = a[0] & 0xFFFFFFFF, a[1] & 0xFFFFFFFF\n        return wrap32({\"and\": x & y, \"or\": x | y, \"xor\": x ^ y}[op])\n    if op == \"shl\":\n        return wrap32((a[0] & 0xFFFFFFFF) << (a[1] & 31))\n    if op == \"shr_s\":\n        return wrap32(a[0] >> (a[1] & 31))\n    if op == \"shr_u\":\n        return wrap32((a[0] & 0xFFFFFFFF) >> (a[1] & 31))\n    if op == \"eqz\":\n        return 1 if a[0] == 0 else 0\n    if op in (\"eq\", \"ne\", \"lt_s\", \"gt_s\", \"le_s\", \"ge_s\",\n              \"lt_u\", \"gt_u\", \"le_u\", \"ge_u\"):\n        x, y = a[0], a[1]\n        if op in (\"lt_u\", \"gt_u\", \"le_u\", \"ge_u\"):\n            x, y = x & 0xFFFFFFFF, y & 0xFFFFFFFF\n        return 1 if {\n            \"eq\": x == y, \"ne\": x != y,\n            \"lt_s\": x < y, \"gt_s\": x > y, \"le_s\": x <= y, \"ge_s\": x >= y,\n            \"lt_u\": x < y, \"gt_u\": x > y, \"le_u\": x <= y, \"ge_u\": x >= y,\n        }[op] else 0\n    raise ValueError(f\"unknown op {op!r}\")\n\n\ndef _val(v: Any, env: Dict[str, int]) -> int:\n    return v if isinstance(v, int) else env[v]\n\n\ndef interpret(fn: Function, args: List[int], step_limit: int = 1_000_000):\n    \"\"\"Direct execution of the SSA IR; the oracle for differential testing.\"\"\"\n    if len(args) != len(fn.params):\n        raise ValueError(\"arity mismatch\")\n    env: Dict[str, int] = dict(zip(fn.params, args))\n    blocks = {b.name: b for b in fn.blocks}\n    cur, pred, steps = fn.entry, None, 0\n    while True:\n        steps += 1\n        if steps > step_limit:\n            raise TimeoutError(\"reference interpreter step limit\")\n        b = blocks[cur]\n        for phi in b.phis:\n            env[phi.dest] = _val(phi.incoming[pred], env)\n        for ins in b.insts:\n            env[ins.dest] = eval_op(ins.op, [_val(a, env) for a in ins.args])\n        t = b.term\n        if isinstance(t, Return):\n            return [_val(v, env) for v in t.values]\n        if isinstance(t, Jump):\n            nxt = t.target\n        else:  # CondBr\n            nxt = t.then_bb if env[t.cond] != 0 else t.else_bb\n        pred, cur = b.name, nxt\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# 3.  Critical-edge splitting\n# =============================================================================\n\n\ndef succ_names(term: Any) -> List[str]:\n    if isinstance(term, Jump):\n        return [term.target]\n    if isinstance(term, CondBr):\n        return [term.then_bb, term.else_bb]\n    return []\n\n\ndef _redirect(term: Any, old: str, new: str) -> None:\n    if isinstance(term, Jump):\n        if term.target == old:\n            term.target = new\n    elif isinstance(term, CondBr):\n        if term.then_bb == old:\n            term.then_bb = new\n        if term.else_bb == old:\n            term.else_bb = new\n\n\ndef split_critical_edges(fn: Function) -> None:\n    \"\"\"\n    A *critical edge* is `u -> v` where u has several successors and v has\n    several predecessors.  Splitting every critical edge gives every phi input\n    a dedicated predecessor block, which is what makes the simple \"append\n    copies to the predecessor\" lowering correct.\n    \"\"\"\n    blocks = {b.name: b for b in fn.blocks}\n    while True:\n        succs = {n: {s for s in succ_names(b.term)} for n, b in blocks.items()}\n        preds: Dict[str, set] = {n: set() for n in blocks}\n        for n, ss in succs.items():\n            for s in ss:\n                preds[s].add(n)\n        progress = False\n        for name, b in list(blocks.items()):\n            if len(succs[name]) <= 1:\n                continue\n            for s in list(succs[name]):\n                if len(preds[s]) <= 1:\n                    continue\n                new = f\"{name}__{s}\"\n                k = 0\n                while new in blocks:\n                    k += 1\n                    new = f\"{name}__{s}_{k}\"\n                blocks[new] = Block(new, [], [], Jump(s))\n                _redirect(b.term, s, new)\n                for phi in blocks[s].phis:\n                    if name in phi.incoming:\n                        phi.incoming[new] = phi.incoming.pop(name)\n                progress = True\n                break\n            if progress:\n                break\n        if not progress:\n            break\n    fn.blocks = list(blocks.values())\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# 4.  Out-of-SSA: parallel copy sequentialisation\n# =============================================================================\n\n\nclass _Fresh:\n    def __init__(self) -> None:\n        self.n = 0\n\n    def __call__(self) -> str:\n        self.n += 1\n        return f\"%tmp{self.n}\"\n\n\ndef sequentialize(moves: List[Tuple[str, Any]], fresh) -> List[Tuple[str, Any]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Turn a *parallel* copy `(d0 := s0, d1 := s1, ...)` into an ordered sequence\n    of scalar moves with identical simultaneous semantics, using temporaries to\n    break cycles (e.g. the swap `a,b := b,a`).\n    \"\"\"\n    moves = [(d, s) for (d, s) in moves if not (d == s)]\n    out: List[Tuple[str, Any]] = []\n    while moves:\n        readers = {s for _, s in moves}\n        ready = next((i for i, m in enumerate(moves) if m[0] not in readers), None)\n        if ready is not None:\n            out.append(moves.pop(ready))\n        else:\n            d, _ = moves[0]\n            tmp = fresh()\n            out.append((tmp, d))                     # tmp := old d\n            moves = [(dd, (tmp if ss == d else ss)) for dd, ss in moves]\n    return out\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# 5.  Lowered (non-SSA) program\n# =============================================================================\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass LBlock:\n    name: str\n    insts: List[Inst]\n    term: Any\n    moves: Dict[str, List[Tuple[str, Any]]]  # successor name -> ordered edge moves\n\n\ndef collect_edge_moves(fn: Function, fresh) -> Dict[Tuple[str, str], list]:\n    raw: Dict[Tuple[str, str], list] = {}\n    for b in fn.blocks:\n        for s in set(succ_names(b.term)):\n            raw[(b.name, s)] = []\n    for b in fn.blocks:\n        for phi in b.phis:\n            for pred, val in phi.incoming.items():\n                raw[(pred, b.name)].append((phi.dest, val))\n    return {k: sequentialize(v, fresh) for k, v in raw.items()}\n\n\ndef build_lowered(fn: Function, edge_moves) -> List[LBlock]:\n    prog = []\n    for b in fn.blocks:\n        mv = {s: edge_moves.get((b.name, s), []) for s in set(succ_names(b.term))}\n        prog.append(LBlock(b.name, list(b.insts), b.term, mv))\n    return prog\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# 6.  Liveness, interference and spill-slot colouring\n# =============================================================================\n\n\ndef _all_vars(fn: Function, prog: List[LBlock]) -> set:\n    vs = set(fn.params)\n    for b in prog:\n        for i in b.insts:\n            vs.add(i.dest)\n            for a in i.args:\n                if isinstance(a, str):\n                    vs.add(a)\n        if isinstance(b.term, CondBr):\n            vs.add(b.term.cond)\n        if isinstance(b.term, Return):\n            for v in b.term.values:\n                if isinstance(v, str):\n                    vs.add(v)\n        for mv in b.moves.values():\n            for d, s in mv:\n                vs.add(d)\n                if isinstance(s, str):\n                    vs.add(s)\n    return vs\n\n\ndef compute_liveness(prog: List[LBlock]):\n    use: Dict[str, set] = {}\n    define: Dict[str, set] = {}\n    succ: Dict[str, List[str]] = {}\n    for b in prog:\n        ss = []\n        if isinstance(b.term, Jump):\n            ss = [b.term.target]\n        elif isinstance(b.term, CondBr):\n            ss = [b.term.then_bb, b.term.else_bb]\n        succ[b.name] = ss\n        reads, writes = set(), set()\n        for i in b.insts:\n            for a in i.args:\n                if isinstance(a, str) and a not in writes:\n                    reads.add(a)\n            writes.add(i.dest)\n        if isinstance(b.term, CondBr):\n            if b.term.cond not in writes:\n                reads.add(b.term.cond)\n        if isinstance(b.term, Return):\n            for v in b.term.values:\n                if isinstance(v, str) and v not in writes:\n                    reads.add(v)\n        # Edge moves are alternatives; treat them simultaneously (conservative).\n        for mv in b.moves.values():\n            for d, s in mv:\n                if isinstance(s, str) and s not in writes:\n                    reads.add(s)\n                writes.add(d)\n        use[b.name], define[b.name] = reads, writes\n\n    live_in = {b.name: set() for b in prog}\n    live_out = {b.name: set() for b in prog}\n    changed = True\n    while changed:\n        changed = False\n        for b in prog:\n            out = set()\n            for s in succ[b.name]:\n                out |= live_in[s]\n            inn = use[b.name] | (out - define[b.name])\n            if out != live_out[b.name] or inn != live_in[b.name]:\n                live_out[b.name], live_in[b.name] = out, inn\n                changed = True\n    return use, define, succ, live_in, live_out\n\n\ndef build_interference(prog, live_in, live_out):\n    edges: Dict[str, set] = {}\n    for b in prog:\n        # Seed with variables live *just before* the edge moves: move sources\n        # plus successor live-in minus move destinations.  Seeding with\n        # live_out alone misses loop-carried values.\n        live = set()\n        if isinstance(b.term, CondBr):\n            live.add(b.term.cond)\n        if isinstance(b.term, Return):\n            live |= {v for v in b.term.values if isinstance(v, str)}\n        for tgt, mv in b.moves.items():\n            defs_m = {d for d, _ in mv}\n            live |= {s for _, s in mv if isinstance(s, str)}\n            live |= (live_in[tgt] - defs_m)\n        for i in reversed(b.insts):\n            for v in live:\n                if v != i.dest:\n                    edges.setdefault(i.dest, set()).add(v)\n                    edges.setdefault(v, set()).add(i.dest)\n            live.discard(i.dest)\n            for a in i.args:\n                if isinstance(a, str):\n                    live.add(a)\n        # per-edge move interference (precise, so coalescing is possible)\n        for tgt, mv in b.moves.items():\n            lv = set(live_in[tgt])\n            for d, s in reversed(mv):\n                for v in lv:\n                    if v != d:\n                        edges.setdefault(d, set()).add(v)\n                        edges.setdefault(v, set()).add(d)\n                lv.discard(d)\n                if isinstance(s, str):\n                    lv.add(s)\n\n    # Parameters live on entry have no defining instruction: clique them.\n    entry_live = sorted(live_in.get(prog[0].name, set()))\n    for i in range(len(entry_live)):\n        for j in range(i + 1, len(entry_live)):\n            a, c = entry_live[i], entry_live[j]\n            edges.setdefault(a, set()).add(c)\n            edges.setdefault(c, set()).add(a)\n    return edges\n\n\ndef color_variables(all_vars: set, edges) -> Dict[str, int]:\n    \"\"\"Greedy graph colouring: colour number == physical wasm local index.\"\"\"\n    colors: Dict[str, int] = {}\n    order = sorted(all_vars, key=lambda v: -len(edges.get(v, ())))\n    for v in order:\n        forbidden = {colors[n] for n in edges.get(v, ()) if n in colors}\n        c = 0\n        while c in forbidden:\n            c += 1\n        colors[v] = c\n    return colors\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# 7.  WAT emission\n# =============================================================================\n\n\nBINARY_OPS = {\n    \"add\": \"i32.add\", \"sub\": \"i32.sub\", \"mul\": \"i32.mul\",\n    \"div_s\": \"i32.div_s\", \"div_u\": \"i32.div_u\",\n    \"rem_s\": \"i32.rem_s\", \"rem_u\": \"i32.rem_u\",\n    \"and\": \"i32.and\", \"or\": \"i32.or\", \"xor\": \"i32.xor\",\n    \"shl\": \"i32.shl\", \"shr_s\": \"i32.shr_s\", \"shr_u\": \"i32.shr_u\",\n    \"eq\": \"i32.eq\", \"ne\": \"i32.ne\",\n    \"lt_s\": \"i32.lt_s\", \"lt_u\": \"i32.lt_u\", \"gt_s\": \"i32.gt_s\", \"gt_u\": \"i32.gt_u\",\n    \"le_s\": \"i32.le_s\", \"le_u\": \"i32.le_u\", \"ge_s\": \"i32.ge_s\", \"ge_u\": \"i32.ge_u\",\n}\nUNARY_OPS = {\"eqz\": \"i32.eqz\", \"clz\": \"i32.clz\", \"ctz\": \"i32.ctz\", \"popcnt\": \"i32.popcnt\"}\n\n\ndef _operand(v: Any, colors) -> str:\n    return (f\"(i32.const {wrap32(v)})\" if isinstance(v, int)\n            else f\"(local.get $l{colors[v]})\")\n\n\ndef emit_inst(i: Inst, colors) -> str:\n    if i.op == \"const\":\n        return f\"(local.set $l{colors[i.dest]} (i32.const {wrap32(i.args[0])}))\"\n    if i.op in UNARY_OPS:\n        return (f\"(local.set $l{colors[i.dest]} \"\n                f\"({UNARY_OPS[i.op]} {_operand(i.args[0], colors)}))\")\n    if i.op in BINARY_OPS:\n        return (f\"(local.set $l{colors[i.dest]} \"\n                f\"({BINARY_OPS[i.op]} {_operand(i.args[0], colors)} \"\n                f\"{_operand(i.args[1], colors)}))\")\n    raise ValueError(f\"cannot emit op {i.op!r}\")\n\n\ndef emit_moves(dst: str, moves, colors, indent: str, out: List[str]) -> None:\n    for d, s in moves:\n        if isinstance(s, str) and colors[d] == colors[s]:\n            continue  # coalesced: the move is a no-op\n        out.append(f\"{indent}(local.set $l{colors[d]} {_operand(s, colors)})\")\n\n\ndef lower_to_wat(fn: Function):\n    \"\"\"Return (wat_text, metadata). Does not mutate `fn`.\"\"\"\n    fn = copy.deepcopy(fn)\n    split_critical_edges(fn)\n\n    fresh = _Fresh()\n    edge_moves = collect_edge_moves(fn, fresh)\n    prog = build_lowered(fn, edge_moves)\n\n    all_vars = _all_vars(fn, prog)\n    use, define, succ, live_in, live_out = compute_liveness(prog)\n    edges = build_interference(prog, live_in, live_out)\n    colors = color_variables(all_vars, edges)\n    nlocals = (max(colors.values()) + 1) if colors else 0\n    n_moves = sum(len(mv) for b in prog for mv in b.moves.values())\n    n_elided = sum(\n        1 for b in prog for mv in b.moves.values() for d, s in mv\n        if isinstance(s, str) and colors[d] == colors[s]\n    )\n\n    idx = {b.name: i for i, b in enumerate(prog)}\n    n = len(prog)\n\n    rets = [b.term.values for b in prog if isinstance(b.term, Return)]\n    arity = len(rets[0]) if rets else 0\n    for r in rets:\n        assert len(r) == arity, \"inconsistent return arity\"\n    results = \" \".join(\"(result i32)\" for _ in range(arity))\n    params = \" \".join(f\"(param $p{i} i32)\" for i in range(len(fn.params)))\n\n    o: List[str] = []\n    o.append(\"(module\")\n    o.append(f\"  (func $f (export \\\"f\\\") {params} {results}\".rstrip())\n    for c in range(nlocals):\n        o.append(f\"    (local $l{c} i32)\")\n    o.append(\"    (local $pc i32)\")\n    for i, pvar in enumerate(fn.params):\n        o.append(f\"    (local.set $l{colors[pvar]} (local.get $p{i}))\")\n    o.append(f\"    (local.set $pc (i32.const {idx[fn.entry]}))\")\n    o.append(\"    (loop $dispatch\")\n\n    # nested dispatch blocks: $b{n} (default) encloses $b{n-1} ... $b0\n    for k in range(n, -1, -1):\n        o.append(f\"      (block $b{k}\")\n    o.append(\"        (br_table \" + \" \".join(f\"$b{k}\" for k in range(n))\n             + f\" $b{n} (local.get $pc))\")\n    for k in range(n):\n        o.append(\"      )\")  # close $b{k}\n        b = prog[k]\n        body: List[str] = []\n        for i in b.insts:\n            body.append(emit_inst(i, colors))\n        t = b.term\n        if isinstance(t, Jump):\n            emit_moves(\"\", b.moves[t.target], colors, \" \" * 8, body)\n            body.append(f\"(local.set $pc (i32.const {idx[t.target]}))\")\n            body.append(\"(br $dispatch)\")\n        elif isinstance(t, CondBr):\n            body.append(f\"(if (local.get $l{colors[t.cond]})\")\n            body.append(\"  (then\")\n            emit_moves(\"\", b.moves[t.then_bb], colors, \" \" * 12, body)\n            body.append(f\"    (local.set $pc (i32.const {idx[t.then_bb]}))\")\n            body.append(\"    (br $dispatch)\")\n            body.append(\"  )\")\n            body.append(\"  (else\")\n            emit_moves(\"\", b.moves[t.else_bb], colors, \" \" * 12, body)\n            body.append(f\"    (local.set $pc (i32.const {idx[t.else_bb]}))\")\n            body.append(\"    (br $dispatch)\")\n            body.append(\"  )\")\n            body.append(\")\")\n        elif isinstance(t, Return):\n            ops = \" \".join(_operand(v, colors) for v in t.values)\n            body.append(f\"(return {ops})\".replace(\"  \", \" \").rstrip())\n        else:\n            raise ValueError(\"bad terminator\")\n        o.extend(\"        \" + line for line in body)\n    o.append(\"      )\")                 # close $b{n} (default)\n    o.append(\"      (unreachable)\")     # invalid pc is semantically impossible\n    o.append(\"    )\")                   # end loop\n    o.append(\"    (unreachable)\")       # make the function end type-check\n    o.append(\"  )\")\n    o.append(\")\")\n    return \"\\n\".join(o) + \"\\n\", {\n        \"colors\": colors, \"nlocals\": nlocals, \"index\": idx,\n        \"n_values\": len(all_vars), \"n_moves\": n_moves, \"n_moves_elided\": n_elided,\n    }\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# 8.  Verification helpers\n# =============================================================================\n\n\ndef wat_to_wasm(wat: str, workdir: str) -> str:\n    os.makedirs(workdir, exist_ok=True)\n    wat_path = os.path.join(workdir, \"m.wat\")\n    wasm_path = os.path.join(workdir, \"m.wasm\")\n    with open(wat_path, \"w\") as f:\n        f.write(wat)\n    subprocess.run([\"wat2wasm\", wat_path, \"-o\", wasm_path],\n                   check=True, capture_output=True)\n    subprocess.run([\"wasm-validate\", wasm_path], check=True, capture_output=True)\n    return wasm_path\n\n\n_NODE_DRIVER = r\"\"\"\nconst fs = require('fs');\nconst bytes = fs.readFileSync(process.argv[2]);\nconst args = JSON.parse(process.argv[3]);\nWebAssembly.instantiate(bytes, {}).then(({instance}) => {\n  try {\n    const r = instance.exports.f(...args);\n    console.log(JSON.stringify({ok: true, result: r}));\n  } catch (e) {\n    console.log(JSON.stringify({ok: false, trap: String(e)}));\n  }\n}).catch(e => { console.log(JSON.stringify({ok: false, error: String(e)})); });\n\"\"\"\n\n\ndef run_wasm(wasm_path: str, args: List[int], workdir: str):\n    driver = os.path.join(workdir, \"run.js\")\n    with open(driver, \"w\") as f:\n        f.write(_NODE_DRIVER)\n    proc = subprocess.run([\"node\", driver, wasm_path, json.dumps(args)],\n                          capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)\n    return json.loads(proc.stdout.strip())\n\n\n# =============================================================================\n# 9.  Differential test harness\n# =============================================================================\n\n\ndef check_function(fn: Function, arg_sets: List[List[int]], verbose: bool = False):\n    wat, meta = lower_to_wat(fn)\n    workdir = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=\"wasmphione\")\n    try:\n        wasm = wat_to_wasm(wat, workdir)\n        for args in arg_sets:\n            expected_exc = None\n            try:\n                expected = interpret(fn, args)\n            except WasmTrap as e:\n                expected_exc = str(e)\n            except TimeoutError:\n                continue\n            got = run_wasm(wasm, args, workdir)\n            if expected_exc is not None:\n                ok = (not got[\"ok\"]) and \"unreachable\" not in got.get(\"trap\", \"\")\n                if not ok:\n                    raise AssertionError(\n                        f\"{fn.name}{args}: expected trap {expected_exc!r}, got {got}\")\n            else:\n                if not got[\"ok\"]:\n                    raise AssertionError(\n                        f\"{fn.name}{args}: expected {expected}, got trap {got}\")\n                exp = expected[0] if len(expected) == 1 else expected\n                if got[\"result\"] != exp:\n                    raise AssertionError(\n                        f\"{fn.name}{args}: expected {expected}, got {got['result']}\")\n        return wat, meta\n    finally:\n        shutil.rmtree(workdir, ignore_errors=True)\n```\n\n### `smoke_test.py` (self-contained; needs only `solution.py`)\n\n```python\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Self-contained smoke test: requires only solution.py and WABT + node.\"\"\"\nfrom solution import (\n    Block, CondBr, Function, Inst, Jump, Phi, Return,\n    check_function, lower_to_wat,\n)\n\n\ndef const(d, n): return Inst(d, \"const\", [n])\ndef add(d, a, b): return Inst(d, \"add\", [a, b])\ndef sub(d, a, b): return Inst(d, \"sub\", [a, b])\ndef mul(d, a, b): return Inst(d, \"mul\", [a, b])\ndef div_s(d, a, b): return Inst(d, \"div_s\", [a, b])\ndef phi(d, **inc): return Phi(d, dict(inc))\n\n\ndef diamond():\n    fn = Function(\"diamond\", [\"%x\"], [\n        Block(\"%entry\", [], [const(\"%z\", 0), Inst(\"%c\", \"ne\", [\"%x\", \"%z\"])],\n              CondBr(\"%c\", \"%t\", \"%f\")),\n        Block(\"%t\", [], [const(\"%two\", 2), mul(\"%y\", \"%x\", \"%two\")], Jump(\"%j\")),\n        Block(\"%f\", [], [const(\"%h\", 100), add(\"%y\", \"%x\", \"%h\")], Jump(\"%j\")),\n        Block(\"%j\", [phi(\"%r\", **{\"%t\": \"%y\", \"%f\": \"%y\"})], [], Return([\"%r\"])),\n    ], \"%entry\")\n    return fn, [[0], [1], [7], [-9]]\n\n\ndef loop_sum():\n    fn = Function(\"loop_sum\", [\"%n\"], [\n        Block(\"%entry\", [], [const(\"%zero\", 0)], Jump(\"%h\")),\n        Block(\"%h\", [phi(\"%sum\", **{\"%entry\": \"%zero\", \"%b\": \"%sum2\"}),\n                     phi(\"%i\", **{\"%entry\": \"%n\", \"%b\": \"%i2\"})],\n              [Inst(\"%c\", \"ne\", [\"%i\", \"%zero\"])], CondBr(\"%c\", \"%b\", \"%e\")),\n        Block(\"%b\", [], [add(\"%sum2\", \"%sum\", \"%i\"), const(\"%one\", 1),\n                         sub(\"%i2\", \"%i\", \"%one\")], Jump(\"%h\")),\n        Block(\"%e\", [], [], Return([\"%sum\"])),\n    ], \"%entry\")\n    return fn, [[0], [1], [5], [10]]\n\n\ndef loop_swap():\n    # phi swap cycle on the back edge\n    fn = Function(\"loop_swap\", [\"%x\"], [\n        Block(\"%entry\", [], [const(\"%one\", 1), const(\"%ten\", 10)], Jump(\"%h\")),\n        Block(\"%h\", [phi(\"%p\", **{\"%entry\": \"%x\", \"%b\": \"%q\"}),\n                     phi(\"%q\", **{\"%entry\": \"%one\", \"%b\": \"%p\"}),\n                     phi(\"%i\", **{\"%entry\": \"%ten\", \"%b\": \"%i2\"})],\n              [Inst(\"%c\", \"ne\", [\"%i\", \"%one\"])], CondBr(\"%c\", \"%b\", \"%e\")),\n        Block(\"%b\", [], [sub(\"%i2\", \"%i\", \"%one\")], Jump(\"%h\")),\n        Block(\"%e\", [], [], Return([\"%p\"])),\n    ], \"%entry\")\n    return fn, [[1], [2], [7], [-3]]\n\n\ndef irreducible():\n    # multi-entry cycle: entry -> b|c ; b->d, c->d, d->b|c\n    fn = Function(\"irreducible\", [\"%x\"], [\n        Block(\"%entry\", [], [const(\"%z\", 0), Inst(\"%c0\", \"ne\", [\"%x\", \"%z\"])],\n              CondBr(\"%c0\", \"%b\", \"%c\")),\n        Block(\"%b\", [phi(\"%vb\", **{\"%entry\": \"%x\", \"%d\": \"%vd\"})],\n              [const(\"%one\", 1), sub(\"%vb2\", \"%vb\", \"%one\")],\n              CondBr(\"%vb2\", \"%d\", \"%e\")),\n        Block(\"%c\", [phi(\"%vc\", **{\"%entry\": \"%x\", \"%d\": \"%vd2\"})],\n              [const(\"%two\", 2), add(\"%vc2\", \"%vc\", \"%two\")],\n              CondBr(\"%vc2\", \"%d\", \"%e\")),\n        Block(\"%d\", [phi(\"%vd\", **{\"%b\": \"%vb2\", \"%c\": \"%vc2\"})],\n              [const(\"%thr\", 3), add(\"%vd2\", \"%vd\", \"%thr\")],\n              CondBr(\"%vd2\", \"%b\", \"%c\")),\n        Block(\"%e\", [], [], Return([\"%x\"])),\n    ], \"%entry\")\n    return fn, [[0], [1], [5], [-3]]\n\n\ndef trap():\n    fn = Function(\"trap\", [\"%x\", \"%n\"], [\n        Block(\"%entry\", [], [], Jump(\"%h\")),\n        Block(\"%h\", [phi(\"%y\", **{\"%entry\": \"%x\", \"%b\": \"%y2\"})],\n              [Inst(\"%c\", \"ne\", [\"%y\", \"%n\"])], CondBr(\"%c\", \"%b\", \"%e\")),\n        Block(\"%b\", [], [div_s(\"%y2\", \"%y\", \"%n\")], Jump(\"%h\")),\n        Block(\"%e\", [], [], Return([\"%y\"])),\n    ], \"%entry\")\n    return fn, [[8, 2], [8, 0], [-8, 2]]\n\n\ndef multivalue():\n    fn = Function(\"multivalue\", [\"%x\"], [\n        Block(\"%entry\", [], [const(\"%z\", 0), Inst(\"%c\", \"gt_s\", [\"%x\", \"%z\"])],\n              CondBr(\"%c\", \"%t\", \"%f\")),\n        Block(\"%t\", [], [const(\"%one\", 1), add(\"%a\", \"%x\", \"%one\"),\n                         sub(\"%b\", \"%x\", \"%one\")], Jump(\"%j\")),\n        Block(\"%f\", [], [const(\"%two\", 2), const(\"%three\", 3),\n                         mul(\"%a\", \"%x\", \"%two\"), mul(\"%b\", \"%x\", \"%three\")], Jump(\"%j\")),\n        Block(\"%j\", [phi(\"%pa\", **{\"%t\": \"%a\", \"%f\": \"%a\"}),\n                     phi(\"%pb\", **{\"%t\": \"%b\", \"%f\": \"%b\"})], [],\n              Return([\"%pa\", \"%pb\"])),\n    ], \"%entry\")\n    return fn, [[0], [1], [2], [-5]]\n\n\ndef multiret():\n    # two values defined in one Return block: catches missing terminator-use edges\n    fn = Function(\"multiret\", [\"%x\"], [\n        Block(\"%entry\", [], [const(\"%z\", 0), Inst(\"%c\", \"gt_s\", [\"%x\", \"%z\"])],\n              CondBr(\"%c\", \"%t\", \"%f\")),\n        Block(\"%t\", [], [const(\"%one\", 1), const(\"%two\", 2)], Return([\"%one\", \"%two\"])),\n        Block(\"%f\", [], [const(\"%m\", -1), const(\"%n\", -2)], Return([\"%m\", \"%n\"])),\n    ], \"%entry\")\n    return fn, [[0], [1], [-5]]\n\n\nSHAPES = [diamond, loop_sum, loop_swap, irreducible, trap, multivalue, multiret]\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    bad = 0\n    for maker in SHAPES:\n        fn, args = maker()\n        try:\n            _, meta = check_function(fn, args)\n            print(f\"PASS {fn.name:12s} locals={meta['nlocals']} \"\n                  f\"moves={meta['n_moves']} elided={meta['n_moves_elided']}\")\n        except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001\n            bad += 1\n            print(f\"FAIL {fn.name:12s} {e}\")\n    print(\"ALL PASS\" if not bad else f\"{bad} FAILURES\")\n    raise SystemExit(1 if bad else 0)\n```\n\n## 5. Verification\n\nEvery module is compiled with **`wat2wasm`**, explicitly re-validated with **`wasm-validate`**, and executed with **Node's `WebAssembly`**. The oracle is the direct SSA interpreter `interpret()` (matching `i32` wrapping and division traps), so both **result values** and **trap/no-trap** behaviour are compared.\n\n```bash\ncd ~/wasm_phi\npython3 smoke_test.py                 # 7 representative shapes, self-contained\npython3 verify.py 500 300             # + 500 random acyclic + 300 random loops\n```\n\nObserved output:\n\n```\nPASS diamond      locals=2 moves=2 elided=2\nPASS loop_sum     locals=4 moves=4 elided=3\nPASS loop_swap    locals=5 moves=7 elided=3\nPASS irreducible  locals=3 moves=6 elided=4\nPASS trap         locals=3 moves=2 elided=0\nPASS multivalue   locals=3 moves=4 elided=4\nPASS multiret     locals=2 moves=0 elided=0\nALL PASS\n\n# verify.py (larger campaign)\n  acyclic random CFGs : 500/500 matched\n  terminating loops   : 300/300 matched\nRESULT: ALL CHECKS PASSED\n```\n\n| Check | Property verified |\n|---|---|\n| `wasm-validate` on every module | emitted code is well-typed structured control flow |\n| `swap`, `loop_swap` | phi cycles sequentialised with temporaries |\n| `diamond`, `loop_sum` | critical edges split; loop-carried liveness correct |\n| `multivalue`, `multiret` | multi-value signatures and phi components |\n| `trap` | `i32.div_s` by zero traps; other inputs match |\n| `irreducible` | multi-entry (irreducible) loops dispatch correctly |\n| 800 randomized differential tests | value **and** trap equivalence on generated CFGs |\n\nThe full fuzz harness (`tests.py`, `verify.py`) and the assembled document (`SOLUTION.md`) are in `~/wasm_phi/`.\n", "evidence": "# Evidence\n- Problem class: wasm-phi-liveness-lowering-structured-control-flow\n- Model: openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash\n- Solved: 2026-11-02T05:07:43.881Z\n- Verification: solution produced by pi in sandbox; see signatures.json\n", "signatures": {"description": "Lower SSA phi nodes with critical edges, irreducible loops, and multi-value WebAssembly blocks into valid structured control flow without changing observable traps or evaluation order. Construct an algorithm that preserves liveness across loop-carried values, minimizes spill code, and emits code accepted by validation for every reducible and irreducible control-flow shape.", "environment": "python3", "language": "python", "model": "openrouter/deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash", "problem_class": "wasm-phi-liveness-lowering-structured-control-flow", "provider": "openrouter", "solved_at": "2026-11-02T05:07:43.891Z", "version": "3"}, "status": "verified", "created_at": "2026-11-02 05:07:51"}
+ {"class_id": 3104, "class": "github-actions-tag-push-no-run-ci-skip", "title": "github-actions-tag-push-no-run-ci-skip", "description": "github-actions-tag-push-no-run-ci-skip", "answer_id": 4839, "language": "go", "environment": "docker", "version": "latest", "solution": "The complete, self-contained solution is saved at `~/SOLUTION.md`. It includes the diagnosis, root cause, fix scripts, prevention guard, and verification steps. Here is the full markdown:\n\n---\n\n# Tag-push CI evidence when the target commit contains a GitHub Actions skip marker\n\n## Summary\n\nA tag push is a `push` event. GitHub suppresses `push` and `pull_request` workflow runs when the pushed commit's message contains one of the skip markers:\n\n`[skip ci]`, `[ci skip]`, `[no ci]`, `[skip actions]`, `[actions skip]`\n\nThere is **no tag-push override**. If a tag points at a skip-marked commit and the workflow has no alternative trigger, `GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}/actions/runs?head_sha=<sha>` will stay at `total_count: 0` forever. The tag still lands; GitHub is not down. The correct response is to stop polling, prove the impossibility, and use one of the workarounds below.\n\nIn the reported case, `c4043b0c` (skip-free) produced run `40202677040`, while `fdcec4c` (subject ends with `[ci skip]`) produced zero runs. That is the expected signature of this problem, not a CI outage.\n\n## Root cause analysis\n\n1. GitHub evaluates skip markers **before** workflow trigger matching for `push` and `pull_request` events.\n2. A tag push is a `push` event. `on.push.tags: ['v*']` does not bypass the marker filter.\n3. The marker is read from the **commit message** of the pushed commit (subject or body), not from the annotated tag message. A skip-free tag annotation does not override a skip-marked commit.\n4. No run means no re-run. GitHub's \"Re-run jobs\" requires an existing run.\n5. Other events only help if the workflow declares them:\n   - `workflow_dispatch` can run on a tag ref.\n   - `repository_dispatch` can target a ref.\n   - `schedule` runs on the default branch, not an arbitrary tag SHA.\n   - `pull_request` events also die on the HEAD commit marker.\n6. Therefore, if the target commit has a marker and none of those alternative triggers exist, zero runs is the correct, expected result. The impossibility proof **is** the coverage finding.\n\n## Step 0 — Diagnose before designing any tag-push CI strategy\n\n### 0.1 Check the target commit message\n\n```bash\nREPO=owner/repo\nTAG=v1.0.0\nSHA=$(git rev-parse \"$TAG^{commit}\")\n\ngit log -1 --format='%H%n%s%n%b' \"$SHA\"\n\nSKIP_RE='\\[(skip[[:space:]-]?ci|ci[[:space:]-]?skip|no[[:space:]-]?ci|skip[[:space:]-]?actions|actions[[:space:]-]?skip)\\]'\nif git log -1 --format=%B \"$SHA\" | grep -Eiq \"$SKIP_RE\"; then\n  echo \"SKIP_MARKER_PRESENT\"\nelse\n  echo \"NO_SKIP_MARKER\"\nfi\n```\n\nThe regex is case-insensitive and also catches hyphenated variants. The official markers use spaces.\n\n### 0.2 Inventory triggers at the target SHA\n\n```bash\ngit ls-tree -r --name-only \"$SHA\" .github/workflows \\\n  | grep -E '\\.ya?ml$' \\\n  | while read -r f; do\n      echo \"--- $f\"\n      git show \"$SHA:$f\" \\\n        | awk '/^on:/{inon=1;print;next} inon && /^[^[:space:]#]/{inon=0} inon{print}' \\\n        | head -60\n    done\n```\n\nLook for `workflow_dispatch`, `repository_dispatch`, `schedule`, `release`, or `create`. If only `push`/`pull_request` exist, no other GitHub event can cover this SHA.\n\n### 0.3 Query runs and checks\n\n```bash\ngh api \"repos/$REPO/actions/runs?head_sha=$SHA&per_page=100\" \\\n  --jq '{total_count, runs: [.workflow_runs[] | {id,name,event,head_branch,status,conclusion,html_url}]}'\n\ngh api \"repos/$REPO/commits/$SHA/check-runs\" \\\n  --jq '{total_count, check_runs: [.check_runs[] | {name,status,conclusion}]}'\n```\n\n### 0.4 Decision table\n\n| Target commit | Alternative trigger at SHA | Existing run | Action |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Skip marker present | None | No | Use Fix A, B, or C below. Do not keep polling. |\n| Skip marker present | `workflow_dispatch` or `repository_dispatch` | No | Dispatch the workflow at the tag ref. |\n| Skip marker present | `schedule` only | No | Schedule runs the default branch, not the tag. Use Fix A/B/C. |\n| No skip marker | Any | No | Normal tag push should run. Investigate tag filter, workflow path, permissions, or Actions being disabled. |\n| No skip marker | Any | Yes | Re-run the existing run. |\n\n### 0.5 One-shot doctor\n\nSave the `ci-tag-doctor.sh` script from the appendix and run:\n\n```bash\nREPO=owner/repo ./ci-tag-doctor.sh v1.0.0\n```\n\nIt prints the commit subject, marker status, workflow `on:` blocks, Actions runs, check runs, and a diagnosis.\n\n## Exact fix\n\n### Fix A — Preferred for new tags: tag a tree-identical, skip-free commit\n\nCreate a new commit with the **same tree** as the target but a skip-free message, then tag that commit. The workflow runs on the new commit; cite tree equivalence to the original.\n\nSave `ci-tag-fix.sh` from the appendix, then:\n\n```bash\n# From a checkout that contains the target commit:\n./ci-tag-fix.sh v1.0.0 v1.0.0-tree\n\n# Or create and push in one shot:\nPUSH=1 ./ci-tag-fix.sh v1.0.0 v1.0.0-tree origin\n```\n\nThe script:\n\n- resolves the target commit and its tree;\n- refuses if the target has no marker (it tells you to tag directly);\n- preserves all parents, including merge commits;\n- creates a new commit with the same tree and a skip-free message;\n- verifies tree equality and marker absence before tagging;\n- creates an annotated tag;\n- optionally pushes it.\n\nChoose a new tag name that matches the workflow's tag filter. For example, `v1.0.0-tree` matches `v*`. If the original tag already exists, do not force-move a release tag unless you explicitly accept rewriting it; use a new tag or Fix C. If signed commits/tags are required, add `-S` to `git commit-tree` and use `git tag -s`.\n\n### Fix B — Exact-SHA local evidence: run the workflow with `act`\n\nThis runs the exact workflow at the exact SHA locally. It is real evidence, but it is not a GitHub Actions run.\n\n```bash\ngit fetch origin\ngit checkout --detach \"$SHA\"\n\nTAG=v1.0.0\njq -n \\\n  --arg ref \"refs/tags/$TAG\" \\\n  --arg sha \"$SHA\" \\\n  --arg repo \"$REPO\" \\\n  --arg msg \"$(git log -1 --format=%B \"$SHA\")\" \\\n  '{ref:$ref,before:\"0000000000000000000000000000000000000000\",after:$sha,head_commit:{id:$sha,message:$msg},repository:{full_name:$repo}}' \\\n  > /tmp/tag-event.json\n\nact -l -e /tmp/tag-event.json\n\nact push \\\n  -W .github/workflows/release.yml \\\n  -e /tmp/tag-event.json \\\n  -P ubuntu-latest=\"${ACT_IMAGE:-catthehacker/ubuntu:act-latest}\" \\\n  --container-architecture linux/amd64\n```\n\nIf the repository ships an act-parity container image, set `ACT_IMAGE` to it. Add secrets with `-s NAME=value` or `--secret-file`. Use `act -l` to confirm the tag-push event is recognized before running.\n\n### Fix C — Existing tag already points at a skip-marked commit: publish the impossibility proof\n\nYou cannot retroactively create a GitHub Actions run for that SHA through the tag push. Generate a report that proves why, and attach it to the release/issue.\n\nSave `ci-tag-proof.sh` from the appendix, then:\n\n```bash\nREPO=owner/repo ./ci-tag-proof.sh v1.0.0\n```\n\nOr generate the report inline:\n\n````bash\nOUT=\"ci-coverage-finding-${TAG//\\//_}.md\"\n{\n  echo \"# CI coverage finding: $REPO $TAG\"\n  echo\n  echo \"- Tag: \\`$TAG\\`\"\n  echo \"- Commit: \\`$SHA\\`\"\n  echo \"- Subject: $(git log -1 --format=%s \"$SHA\")\"\n  echo \"- Skip marker: present\"\n  echo\n  echo '## Commit message'\n  echo '```'\n  git log -1 --format='%H%n%an <%ae>%n%ad%n%n%B' \"$SHA\"\n  echo '```'\n  echo\n  echo '## Workflow triggers at this SHA'\n  echo '```'\n  git ls-tree -r --name-only \"$SHA\" .github/workflows \\\n    | grep -E '\\.ya?ml$' \\\n    | while read -r f; do\n        echo \"--- $f\"\n        git show \"$SHA:$f\" \\\n          | awk '/^on:/{inon=1;print;next} inon && /^[^[:space:]#]/{inon=0} inon{print}' \\\n          | head -60\n      done\n  echo '```'\n  echo\n  echo '## GitHub Actions runs for this SHA'\n  echo '```json'\n  gh api \"repos/$REPO/actions/runs?head_sha=$SHA&per_page=100\" \\\n    --jq '{total_count, runs: [.workflow_runs[] | {id,name,event,head_branch,status,conclusion,html_url}]}'\n  echo '```'\n  echo\n  echo '## Check runs for this SHA'\n  echo '```json'\n  gh api \"repos/$REPO/commits/$SHA/check-runs\" \\\n    --jq '{total_count, check_runs: [.check_runs[] | {name,status,conclusion}]}'\n  echo '```'\n  echo\n  echo '## Conclusion'\n  echo\n  echo 'The tag landed, but GitHub Actions cannot create a run for this SHA because the commit message contains a GitHub Actions skip marker and no alternative trigger exists. This is an impossibility proof, not a CI failure.'\n} > \"$OUT\"\n````\n\n### Fix D — If the workflow already declares `workflow_dispatch`\n\n`workflow_dispatch` is not a `push` event, so the skip marker does not suppress it. If the workflow declares it, dispatch at the tag ref:\n\n```bash\ngh workflow run release.yml --ref \"$TAG\"\ngh run list --workflow release.yml --limit 5\n```\n\nIf the workflow does not declare `workflow_dispatch`, add it on the default branch as a future escape hatch. Do not rely on `schedule` to cover a specific tag SHA.\n\n## Prevention\n\n- **Never cut release tags from `[ci skip]` bookkeeping/log commits.** This is the primary fix.\n- Add a pre-tag/pre-push guard. A workflow on tag push cannot enforce this because the tag push itself is skipped. Use a client-side guard, a GitHub ruleset with required checks (if available), or a GitHub App/webhook.\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\nSKIP_RE='\\[(skip[[:space:]-]?ci|ci[[:space:]-]?skip|no[[:space:]-]?ci|skip[[:space:]-]?actions|actions[[:space:]-]?skip)\\]'\nfor ref in \"$@\"; do\n  sha=$(git rev-parse \"$ref^{commit}\")\n  if git log -1 --format=%B \"$sha\" | grep -Eiq \"$SKIP_RE\"; then\n    echo \"REFUSING to tag/push $ref: commit $sha has a CI skip marker\" >&2\n    exit 1\n  fi\ndone\n```\n\n- Use skip markers only on commits that will never be tagged or released, and keep release branches free of them.\n- Add `workflow_dispatch` to release workflows as a manual escape hatch:\n\n```yaml\non:\n  push:\n    tags: ['v*']\n  workflow_dispatch:\n```\n\n- Do not rely on `schedule` to cover a specific tag SHA; schedule runs on the default branch.\n\n## Verification\n\n### Local verification of Fix A (no GitHub needed)\n\nSave `test-ci-tag-fix.sh` from the appendix and run:\n\n```bash\n./test-ci-tag-fix.sh\n```\n\nExpected output:\n\n```text\nPASS\ntarget=<original-skip-marked-sha>\nnew=<tree-identical-sha>\ntree=<same-tree-for-both>\nnew_subject=ci: trigger workflow for <original-skip-marked-sha> (tree-identical; no skip marker)\n```\n\nThe test creates a temporary repository, makes a commit with `[ci skip]`, runs `ci-tag-fix.sh`, and asserts that the new commit has the same tree and no skip marker.\n\n### GitHub verification after pushing the new tag\n\n```bash\nNEW_TAG=\"${TAG}-tree\"\nNEW_SHA=$(git rev-parse \"$NEW_TAG^{commit}\")\n\n# 1. Tag landed\ngit ls-remote --tags origin \"refs/tags/$NEW_TAG\"\n\n# 2. Tree is identical to the original\ntest \"$(git rev-parse \"$TAG^{tree}\")\" = \"$(git rev-parse \"$NEW_TAG^{tree}\")\" \\\n  && echo \"TREE_IDENTICAL\"\n\n# 3. New commit has no skip marker\ngit log -1 --format=%B \"$NEW_SHA\" | grep -Eiq \"$SKIP_RE\" \\\n  && echo \"BAD: marker present\" \\\n  || echo \"NO_SKIP_MARKER\"\n\n# 4. Poll for the run (bounded)\nfor i in $(seq 1 30); do\n  n=$(gh api \"repos/$REPO/actions/runs?head_sha=$NEW_SHA&per_page=1\" --jq .total_count)\n  echo \"poll $i: total_count=$n\"\n  [ \"$n\" -gt 0 ] && break\n  sleep 10\ndone\n\n# 5. Inspect the run\ngh api \"repos/$REPO/actions/runs?head_sha=$NEW_SHA&per_page=100\" \\\n  --jq '.workflow_runs[] | {id,name,event,head_branch,status,conclusion,html_url}'\n```\n\nExpected: the tag exists, the tree comparison prints `TREE_IDENTICAL`, the marker check prints `NO_SKIP_MARKER`, and the poll eventually reports `total_count > 0` with a run whose `head_sha` is `$NEW_SHA`.\n\n### Controlled comparison (proves the marker is the cause)\n\nPush one probe tag at a skip-free commit and one at the skip-marked commit, then compare:\n\n```bash\nCLEAN_SHA=<skip-free-sha>\nSKIP_SHA=<skip-marked-sha>\n\ngit tag -a v0.0.0-probe-clean \"$CLEAN_SHA\" -m \"probe\"\ngit tag -a v0.0.0-probe-skip  \"$SKIP_SHA\"  -m \"probe\"\ngit push origin v0.0.0-probe-clean v0.0.0-probe-skip\n\nfor sha in \"$CLEAN_SHA\" \"$SKIP_SHA\"; do\n  echo \"$sha: $(gh api \"repos/$REPO/actions/runs?head_sha=$sha&per_page=1\" --jq .total_count)\"\ndone\n```\n\nExpected: the skip-free SHA has at least one run; the skip-marked SHA has zero. This is the controlled comparison that isolates the commit-message marker as the cause.\n\n### Verification of Fix C (impossibility proof)\n\n```bash\ngh api \"repos/$REPO/actions/runs?head_sha=$SHA&per_page=100\" --jq .total_count\n# expected: 0\n\ngh api \"repos/$REPO/commits/$SHA/check-runs\" --jq .total_count\n# expected: 0\n\ngit log -1 --format=%B \"$SHA\" | grep -Eiq \"$SKIP_RE\" && echo \"marker present\"\n```\n\nThe report generated by `ci-tag-proof.sh` captures all three facts and states the conclusion: the tag landed, but GitHub Actions cannot create a run for this SHA because of the skip marker and the absence of an alternative trigger.\n\n## Appendix — scripts\n\n### `ci-tag-doctor.sh`\n\n```bash\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n\nSKIP_RE='\\[(skip[[:space:]-]?ci|ci[[:space:]-]?skip|no[[:space:]-]?ci|skip[[:space:]-]?actions|actions[[:space:]-]?skip)\\]'\nREF=${1:-HEAD}\nREPO=${REPO:-}\nif [ -z \"$REPO\" ] && command -v gh >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n  REPO=$(gh repo view --json nameWithOwner -q .nameWithOwner 2>/dev/null || true)\nfi\nTARGET=$(git rev-parse --verify \"$REF^{commit}\")\nSUBJECT=$(git log -1 --format=%s \"$TARGET\")\nBODY=$(git log -1 --format=%B \"$TARGET\")\nif printf '%s\\n' \"$BODY\" | grep -Eiq \"$SKIP_RE\"; then SKIP=yes; else SKIP=no; fi\n\nprintf 'repo: %s\\n' \"${REPO:-<unknown>}\"\nprintf 'ref: %s\\n' \"$REF\"\nprintf 'sha: %s\\n' \"$TARGET\"\nprintf 'subject: %s\\n' \"$SUBJECT\"\nprintf 'skip_marker: %s\\n' \"$SKIP\"\nprintf '\\nworkflow files at %s:\\n' \"$TARGET\"\nmapfile -t WF < <(git ls-tree -r --name-only \"$TARGET\" .github/workflows 2>/dev/null | grep -E '\\.ya?ml$' || true)\nif [ ${#WF[@]} -eq 0 ]; then\n  echo '  (none)'\nelse\n  for f in \"${WF[@]}\"; do\n    echo \"--- $f\"\n    git show \"$TARGET:$f\" | awk '\n      /^on:/ {inon=1; print; next}\n      inon && /^[^[:space:]#]/ {inon=0}\n      inon {print}\n    ' | head -60\n  done\nfi\n\nprintf '\\nGitHub Actions runs for head_sha=%s:\\n' \"$TARGET\"\nif [ -n \"$REPO\" ] && command -v gh >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n  gh api \"repos/$REPO/actions/runs?head_sha=$TARGET&per_page=100\" \\\n    --jq '{total_count, runs: [.workflow_runs[] | {id, name, event, head_branch, status, conclusion, html_url}]}' \\\n    2>/dev/null || echo '  (gh query failed; check auth/network)'\n  printf '\\nCheck runs for %s:\\n' \"$TARGET\"\n  gh api \"repos/$REPO/commits/$TARGET/check-runs\" \\\n    --jq '{total_count, check_runs: [.check_runs[] | {name, status, conclusion}]}' \\\n    2>/dev/null || echo '  (gh query failed; check auth/network)'\nelse\n  echo '  (skipped: no repo or gh)'\nfi\n\nif [ \"$SKIP\" = yes ]; then\n  cat <<'EOF'\n\nDIAGNOSIS: The target commit message contains a GitHub Actions skip marker.\nGitHub evaluates this marker for push events, including tag pushes. No run will\nbe created for this SHA by an on.push.tags workflow, and there is no tag-push\noverride. Re-run is impossible without an existing run. Use one of:\n  A) tag a tree-identical, skip-free commit (ci-tag-fix.sh)\n  B) run the workflow locally with act at this exact SHA\n  C) if the release tag already